title: Multi-origin batches paid by their submitter
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    This PR adds `pallet-utility-ext`, which allows batching calls with different origins in one
    signed transaction. The origins can be custom, they don't have to be
    `SystemOrigin::Signed(AccountId)`. This is done by supplying a number of batch items
    consisting of a call, its inner `TransactionExtension` pipeline and its expected origin to
    `pallet-utility-ext::batch_multi_origin` or `force_batch_multi_origin`.

    The batch is a regular signed transaction of its submitter. Its call applies the items in
    sequence: each item runs its inner pipeline's `validate` and `prepare`, is dispatched and runs
    its pipeline's `post_dispatch` before the next item starts, exactly as if it were a
    transaction of its own included right after the previous item. An invalid item is not applied
    and its origin pays nothing, like an invalid transaction; an item whose call fails keeps its
    pipeline's effects (nonce, fee) while the call's effects are reverted, like a failed
    transaction. Batches are not atomic: `batch_multi_origin` stops at the first item which is
    invalid or fails and keeps the items before it, `force_batch_multi_origin` applies every
    item regardless. Items are not validated in the transaction pool, only the submitter's
    transaction is.

    The submitter pays for the batch overhead, the items' pipelines and the transaction's
    length, the items pay for their own calls (call weight plus base extrinsic fee). Items are
    signed over an implication binding them to the submitter, the submitter's nonce, the batch
    mode, their position and the other items, so an item cannot be applied elsewhere.

    Enabling this feature means configuring `pallet-utility-ext` in your runtime. Importantly, the
    pallet requires:

    - A maximum number of calls allowed in a single batch. This should generally be a low figure
      (e.g. 8 calls).
    - A call filter for which calls are allowed to be part of multi origin batches.
    - The inner `TransactionExtension` pipeline carried by each item. This is different than the
      runtime's overarching `TransactionExtension` and is meant to be a subset of the extensions
      there, with the signature verification first and without the extensions which are supposed
      to run only once per transaction (e.g. `CheckWeight`, `WeightReclaim`). With the
      `runtime-benchmarks` feature, a benchmark helper building items of that type is required
      as well.

    The pallet's `MultiOriginBatch` transaction extension goes in the overarching
    `TransactionExtension` after `CheckNonce` and before the fee payment extension. It
    authorizes the batch calls and charges the submitter for their share through the runtime's
    `OnChargeTransaction`, keeping the withdrawn fee out of the transient fee credit of
    `pallet-transaction-payment` while the items run, so the fee payment extension skips
    batches and the items' own fee payments find that credit empty. An item whose dispatch
    returns `Pays::No` is free for the submitter too. Calls relying on that credit belonging to
    the transaction's signer, today the Ethereum transaction calls of `pallet_revive`, must be
    kept out of batches by the call filter.

    For wallets, the item pipeline type is part of the pallet's metadata and the
    `MultiOriginBatchApi` runtime API returns the pipeline's explicit and implicit data per
    extension, the payload an item's origin authorizes and the submitter's share of the fee.
    `TransactionExtension` gains `explicit_parts` and `implicit_parts`, with default
    implementations, giving the encoded data of each extension of a pipeline in metadata order.
    `TransactionValidityError` and its variants now derive `DecodeWithMemTracking`.
crates:
- name: pallet-utility-ext
  bump: major
- name: sp-runtime
  bump: minor
- name: polkadot-sdk
  bump: minor
